---
title: Remove Packages
order: 2
description: -R, -Rs, -Rns, -Rdd, orphan cleanup
tags: [pacman, remove, uninstall, orphan]
---

Removing packages and cleaning up orphaned dependencies.

@code bash
# ---- basic removal ----

pacman -R package                         # remove package only
pacman -Rs package                        # remove + unneeded dependencies
pacman -Rns package                       # remove + deps + config files (clean)
pacman -Rn package                        # remove + config files, keep deps
pacman -Rsu package                       # remove + deps, skip if others need them

@text
`-Rns` is the recommended removal command. It removes the package, its
orphaned dependencies, and any backup config files (pacnew/pacsave).

@code bash
# ---- force removal (dangerous) ----

# skip dependency checks (breaks things!)
pacman -Rdd package                       # remove even if others depend on it

@text
Using `-Rdd` can leave your system in a broken state. Only use it if you
know exactly what you are doing and plan to immediately install a
replacement.

@code bash
# ---- orphan management ----

# list orphaned packages (installed as deps, nothing depends on them)
pacman -Qdt

# list all unrequired packages (orphans + optional deps)
pacman -Qdtt

# remove all orphans
pacman -Rns $(pacman -Qdtq)

# remove all orphans (safe version, handles empty result)
orphans=$(pacman -Qdtq)
if [ -n "$orphans" ]; then
    pacman -Rns $orphans
else
    echo "No orphans found"
fi

@text
After removing packages, check for orphans periodically. Dependencies
pulled in by removed packages often linger. A clean system has no output
from `pacman -Qdt`.

@code bash
# ---- check what depends on a package before removing ----

pacman -Qi package | grep "Required By"   # direct dependents
pactree -r package                        # full reverse dependency tree
